{
  "page": 58,
  "lines": [
    {
      "text": "326",
      "bbox": [
        42.70989990234375,
        39.52751922607422,
        57.665863037109375,
        47.82143783569336
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Discussion on the Paper by Andrieu, Doucet and Holenstein",
      "bbox": [
        78.57550048828125,
        39.52751922607422,
        316.3913879394531,
        47.82143783569336
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "examples that are considered in the paper. In particular, does it hold for various signal-to-noise ratio",
      "bbox": [
        42.70989990234375,
        57.378700256347656,
        438.2314147949219,
        65.6905517578125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "combinations of σ and σx?",
      "bbox": [
        42.70989990234375,
        66.5881118774414,
        143.35855102539062,
        75.65313720703125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Adam M. Johansen and John A. D. Aston (University of Warwick, Coventry)",
      "bbox": [
        42.709815979003906,
        87.2663345336914,
        327.6306457519531,
        95.57818603515625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "We congratulate the authors on an exciting paper which combines the novel idea of incorporating sequen-",
      "bbox": [
        42.7098388671875,
        97.22891998291016,
        438.22222900390625,
        105.540771484375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "tial Monte Carlo proposals within Markov chain Monte Carlo samplers with a synthesis of ideas from",
      "bbox": [
        42.7098388671875,
        107.1915054321289,
        438.22235107421875,
        115.50335693359375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "disparate areas. It is clear that the paper is a substantial advance in Monte Carlo methodology and is of",
      "bbox": [
        42.7098388671875,
        117.15409088134766,
        438.2304382324219,
        125.4659423828125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "substantially greater value than a collection of its constituent parts.",
      "bbox": [
        42.7098388671875,
        127.1166763305664,
        296.81756591796875,
        135.42852783203125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "However, one constituent which has received little attention in the literature seems to us to be interesting:",
      "bbox": [
        51.676239013671875,
        137.0792694091797,
        438.2229309082031,
        145.39111328125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "although it is computationally rather expensive to do so, equations (27)–(28) suggest that it is possible to",
      "bbox": [
        42.7098388671875,
        147.04185485839844,
        438.2222595214844,
        155.35369873046875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "obtain samples which characterize the path space distribution well, at least in the case of mixing dynamic",
      "bbox": [
        42.7098388671875,
        157.0044403076172,
        438.22113037109375,
        165.3162841796875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "systems when we are interested in marginal distributions of bounded dimension, albeit at the cost of",
      "bbox": [
        42.7098388671875,
        166.96702575683594,
        438.22674560546875,
        175.27886962890625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "running an independent sequential Monte Carlo algorithm for every sample. In practice some reuse of",
      "bbox": [
        42.7098388671875,
        176.9296112060547,
        438.2302551269531,
        185.241455078125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "samples is likely to be possible.",
      "bbox": [
        42.7098388671875,
        186.8921661376953,
        159.18338012695312,
        195.20401000976562
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Typically, such a strategy might be dismissed (perhaps correctly) as being of prohibitive computational",
      "bbox": [
        51.676239013671875,
        196.85472106933594,
        438.2321472167969,
        205.16656494140625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "cost. However, in an era in which Monte Carlo algorithms whose time cost scales superlinearly in the",
      "bbox": [
        42.7098388671875,
        206.81727600097656,
        438.2267150878906,
        215.12911987304688
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "number of samples employed are common, might there be other situations in which this strategy ﬁnds a",
      "bbox": [
        42.7098388671875,
        216.7798309326172,
        438.23394775390625,
        225.0916748046875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "role?",
      "bbox": [
        42.7098388671875,
        226.7423858642578,
        60.61573791503906,
        235.05422973632812
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "A rather naive approach to smoothing, for example, would be to employ an ensemble of independent",
      "bbox": [
        51.676239013671875,
        236.70494079589844,
        438.226806640625,
        245.01678466796875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "particle ﬁlters and to sample one trajectory from each independent ﬁlter. For simplicity, consider employing",
      "bbox": [
        42.7098388671875,
        246.66749572753906,
        438.2303466796875,
        254.97933959960938
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "a bootstrap ﬁlter in the univariate case, with fθ.xn|xn−1/=N.xn;xn−1, 1/ and gθ.yn|xn/=N.yn|xn, 1/. To",
      "bbox": [
        42.7098388671875,
        255.87835693359375,
        438.22216796875,
        265.65765380859375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "assess performance, consider the estimated covariance of Xn:n+1 (and the determinant of that covariance, to",
      "bbox": [
        42.709747314453125,
        266.5940856933594,
        438.2219543457031,
        276.2510070800781
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "provide a compact summary). Fig. 18 shows covariance estimates obtained by using a 100-ﬁlter ensemble,",
      "bbox": [
        42.709503173828125,
        276.55670166015625,
        438.2245178222656,
        284.8685607910156
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "each of 100 particles, a single particle ﬁlter of equal cost (using 10000 particles) and the exact solution",
      "bbox": [
        42.709503173828125,
        286.5192565917969,
        438.22027587890625,
        294.83111572265625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(Kalman smoothing). This illustrates the degeneracy and consequent failure to represent sample path",
      "bbox": [
        42.709503173828125,
        296.4818115234375,
        438.22442626953125,
        304.7936706542969
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "variability of a single ﬁlter adequately and contrasts it with the estimate obtained by using an ensemble",
      "bbox": [
        42.709503173828125,
        306.4443664550781,
        438.222900390625,
        314.7562255859375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
